\title{Regression Learner for L0-Regularized Best Subset Selection}
\description{
Sparse linear regression with L0, L0L1 or L0L2 penalties, fitted by
coordinate descent and local combinatorial search with
\code{\link[L0Learn:L0Learn.cvfit]{L0Learn::L0Learn.cvfit()}}.
The penalty parameters \code{lambda} and \code{gamma} are chosen by cross-validation
inside \code{L0Learn::L0Learn.cvfit()}, then the pair with the smallest mean
cross-validated error is stored in the model and used for prediction.

The loss is always the squared error.
Features must be \code{"integer"} or \code{"numeric"}, and missing values are not
supported by L0Learn.
}
\section{Initial parameter values}{

All parameters have the tag \code{"train"} and are passed to
\code{\link[L0Learn:L0Learn.cvfit]{L0Learn::L0Learn.cvfit()}}, except \code{selection}, which is used by the learner
itself.
Parameters that are not set explicitly keep the defaults of L0Learn.
\code{nGamma}, \code{gammaMax} and \code{gammaMin} only matter for the \code{"L0L1"} and
\code{"L0L2"} penalties.
}

\section{Cross-validation folds}{

The folds are built by \code{L0Learn::L0Learn.cvfit()}, which cannot split every
number of rows \code{n} into every number of folds \code{nFolds}.
When \code{ceiling(n / nFolds) * (nFolds - 1) >= n} it either fails with an
out-of-bounds error or returns only \code{NaN} cross-validation errors.
For example \code{nFolds = 10} fails on the 32 rows of \code{mlr3::tsk("mtcars")}.
The learner then stops with an informative error, and another value of
\code{nFolds} must be chosen.
}

\section{Random numbers}{

\code{L0Learn::L0Learn.cvfit()} calls \code{set.seed(seed)}.
The learner restores the state of the random number generator after
training, so fitting it does not change the random numbers drawn afterwards.
The cross-validation folds are the same for a given \code{seed}, whose default
is \code{1}.
}

\section{Choice of lambda and gamma}{

With \code{selection = "min"} (the default) the learner uses the pair with the
smallest mean cross-validated error.
When several values are tied, the one furthest along the \code{lambda} path
(the smallest \code{lambda}, usually the largest support) is used.

With \code{selection = "1se"} the learner keeps the \code{gamma} of that minimum, then
takes the model on the \code{lambda} path with the smallest support among those
whose mean cross-validated error is within one standard error of the minimum.
The standard error is the standard deviation of the fold errors at the
minimum divided by \code{sqrt(nFolds)}.
If it is not available the minimum is used, with a warning.
This usually gives a smaller support and a more stable choice of features.
}

\section{Bounds and custom penalty grids}{

\code{lows} and \code{highs} are single numbers that bound every coefficient, with
\verb{lows <= 0 <= highs} and \code{lows < highs}.
Bounds are not supported by \code{algorithm = "CDPSI"}.
\code{lambdaGrid} is a list of strictly decreasing positive numeric vectors.
For \code{penalty = "L0"} it must contain exactly one vector, and for \code{"L0L1"}
and \code{"L0L2"} it contains one vector per value of \code{gamma}.
When it is set, \code{nLambda} is ignored by L0Learn and \code{nGamma} is replaced by
the number of vectors.
L0Learn can drop the last values of a grid whose support exceeds
\code{maxSuppSize}, so the stored path may be shorter than the grid.
\code{excludeFirstK} cannot be larger than the number of features, because
L0Learn would abort the R session.
}

\section{Model}{

After training, \code{learner$model} is a list with these elements:
\itemize{
\item \code{fit}: the \code{"L0LearnCV"} object returned by \code{\link[L0Learn:L0Learn.cvfit]{L0Learn::L0Learn.cvfit()}}.
\item \code{gamma_index}, \code{lambda_index}: the position of the selected pair in the
regularization path of \code{fit}.
\item \code{lambda}, \code{gamma}: the selected values.
\item \code{support_size}: the number of non-zero coefficients of the selected model.
\item \code{cv_mean}, \code{cv_sd}: the mean and standard deviation, across folds, of the
cross-validated error at the selected pair.
}
}

\section{Selected features}{

\code{learner$selected_features()} returns the names of the features with a
non-zero coefficient at the selected \verb{(lambda, gamma)} pair.
}

\examples{
library(mlr3L0Learn)
learner <- mlr3::lrn("regr.L0Learn", penalty = "L0", nFolds = 5L)
task <- mlr3::tsk("mtcars")
learner$train(task)
learner$selected_features()
prediction <- learner$predict(task)
prediction$score(mlr3::msr("regr.mse"))

}
